---
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
---
<Layout title="Threat model — Civic Ledger">
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Threat model</h1>
  <p class="mt-2 max-w-prose text-neutral-700">
    What someone could learn about you from this site, who that someone could
    be, and what is actually promised. Written before accounts exist, so that
    the description comes first and the collection second.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Today, with no accounts</h2>
  <ul class="mt-2 max-w-prose list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm text-neutral-700">
    <li>
      There is no account system yet, so nothing you do here creates a record
      about you on the operator's side.
    </li>
    <li>
      The site is delivered by Cloudflare, which receives the requests your
      browser makes. That is a record of what you read, held by a company the
      operator does not control. Every website has a host and this is what
      having one means.
    </li>
    <li>
      This site's own code stores nothing in your browser — no cookies, no
      saved data. That is a statement about today's code, not a promise about
      future code, and it does not cover anything Cloudflare may set.
    </li>
    <li>
      On the members explorer, what you type into the search box is copied
      into the page address as you type. It is not sent anywhere by itself,
      but it travels with the address — so it is in your history, and in any
      link you bookmark or share. The same is true of the member comparison
      page, whose address carries the members you chose.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">When accounts ship</h2>
  <p class="mt-1 max-w-prose text-sm text-neutral-700">
    Accounts are planned, not built. When they arrive, the site will hold
    these things about you, and <strong>the operator can read all of them</strong>:
  </p>
  <ul class="mt-2 max-w-prose list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-neutral-700">
    <li>Who you are: your email address, whether it has been confirmed, a scrambled form of your password, and when you signed up.</li>
    <li>What you follow: which bills, topics and members, and how often you want to hear about them.</li>
    <li>Your settings and your answers to anything the site asks you.</li>
    <li>Which alerts have already gone to you, so the same one is not sent twice.</li>
    <li>Where to reach you: your email address, and a phone number only if that is ever added.</li>
    <li>A public profile, only if you choose to make one. Published means public.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-2 max-w-prose text-sm text-neutral-700">
    An earlier design for this site scrambled your answers on your own device,
    so that even the operator could not read them. That was given up on
    2026-09-23, for a plain reason: a server that cannot read your list of
    followed bills cannot send you alerts about them. Alerts were the point,
    so the scrambling went.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">What a legal demand would produce</h2>
  <p class="mt-1 max-w-prose text-sm text-neutral-700">
    Once accounts exist: everything in the list above, in readable form. Your
    email address, what you follow, your settings and answers, and where you
    get notified. Not scrambled, not partial. Anyone who can compel the
    operator to hand over data gets the real thing.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-2 max-w-prose text-sm text-neutral-700">
    Separately, and outside the operator's reach: whoever delivers the site
    and whoever sends the email hold their own records, and can be compelled
    on their own.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">What is not promised</h2>
  <p class="mt-1 max-w-prose text-sm text-neutral-700">
    This section exists because the site used to promise these things and the
    promises could not be kept. As of 2026-10-05 the operator does
    <strong>not</strong> promise that requests go unlogged, that no network
    address of yours is stored, that no cookie is ever set, or that storage
    stays limited to the list above. Features this site is being built toward
    need to keep a record of what you follow. A promise that cannot survive
    the next feature is worth less than an accurate description, so you are
    getting the description.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">What is promised</h2>
  <ul class="mt-2 max-w-prose list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm text-neutral-700">
    <li>
      <strong>Your data is never sold.</strong> This is the one standing
      promise. It holds whatever gets built, because selling your data would
      be a decision by the operator rather than something the software could
      do on its own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Deletion is real deletion.</strong> When accounts exist, asking
      for your account to be deleted removes its rows, rather than flagging
      them as hidden.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing published can single you out.</strong> Any figure this
      site publishes that is derived from reader data has to cover at least
      50 people. Below that, the number is not published at all.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Found something wrong here?</h2>
  <p class="mt-1 max-w-prose text-sm text-neutral-700">
    This page is a factual claim like any other on the site, and it can be
    wrong. The <a href="/corrections" class="underline hover:text-neutral-900">corrections page</a>
    explains how to report it and how fixes are recorded.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-8 text-xs text-neutral-500">
    Last reviewed 2026-10-05. The sections describing accounts will be
    rewritten as each feature actually ships, before it collects anything.
  </p>
</Layout>
